// The one *runtime* entry point that can reach a forwarding generic fn by name without pinning its
// instantiation: `invoke(name, args)`, which resolves the callee from the global slot at run time,
// so no static callee type exists for the checker to record type arguments against.
//
// It aborts naming the callee, on the same reasoning as `forward_fn_not_value.noe` refuses the bare
// reference statically — the hidden type-argument slot would be missing either way. And it aborts
// with the RIGHT complaint: `invoke` reckons arity over the value parameters, so a forwarding
// callee's slots do not inflate the count and this does not report "takes 2 argument(s) but 1 were
// supplied" about a function the source wrote with one.
//
// `invoke` is otherwise a fallible surface (a resolution miss is a `Result.Err`), and this is
// deliberately not that: an unknown name or a wrong argument count is the caller's mistake to
// handle, while a missing instantiation is a hole in what `invoke` can express at all.
// expect: stdout "direct ok"
// expect: error E0058 at 34:1
// expect: exit 1
use std.json
use std.json.JsonError

struct Order {
    id: int
}

fn load<T>(text: string): Result<T, JsonError> {
    return json.try_parse::<T>(text)
}

direct = load::<Order>("{\"id\": 1}")
echo match direct {
    Ok(v) => "direct ok",
    Err(e) => "unreached",
}

invoke("load", ["{\"id\": 1}"])
